Raw dev notes -- Side Projects: From RTL Tweaks to Pi Security Deep Dives

Alright, another couple of weeks, another grab bag of side project work. It's always a balancing act trying to give these things enough attention, but even small wins feel good.

First up, there's my little Hebrew RTL extension. This thing exists because dealing with right-to-left text, especially Hebrew, in standard web content editors can be a nightmare. It's a small utility I built to make that process less painful, specifically for a composer environment. It's one of those "scratch your own itch" projects that ended up being surprisingly useful for others too. This past period, I literally just bumped the version number on its landing page hero badge to v2.2.5. It's a tiny maintenance task, but it's important to keep the public-facing info current so users know they're getting the latest.

Then there's VisionMaker, which is my ongoing deep dive into home lab security, specifically around Raspberry Pis. I'm trying to figure out how to apply enterprise-grade security principles and tools to my personal IoT devices. It's less about building a new app and more about understanding and hardening my own infrastructure. The goal is to learn how these big security platforms actually work in practice, not just in theory, and see how far I can push them on resource-constrained devices like a Pi 5.

What I actually did for VisionMaker this time was purely documentation. I spent a good chunk of time researching and testing endpoint security agents for the Pi 5. The commit was literally "docs: Pi 5 endpoint security agents (runZero works, Falcon RFM)". This means I was looking into tools like runZero for asset inventory and another well-known EDR solution. The big takeaway, and honestly, the surprising part, was that these agents *do* work on the Pi 5's ARM architecture. I went into it expecting a lot more friction, compatibility issues, or just outright failure. It's not a simple install-and-forget, mind you, but the fact that they can be made to function is a huge step.

The hard part isn't necessarily getting them to run, but understanding the telemetry they provide, configuring them optimally for a low-power device, and then making sense of the sheer volume of data. It's a rabbit hole of documentation, configuration files, and trying to translate enterprise-level concepts to a home lab. It really highlights how much goes into securing even a single endpoint, let alone a whole fleet. The "docs" commit itself is a testament to the fact that sometimes the most valuable work isn't writing code, but understanding and documenting complex systems.

Reflecting on it, giving a quick update to the Hebrew extension felt like checking in on an old friend. It's a reminder that even small, niche tools can have a long life and still need a little love. VisionMaker, on the other hand, is a completely different beast. It's pushing me into areas of systems security and operations that are new and challenging. It feels like I'm leveling up my understanding of security beyond just application-layer vulnerabilities. It's slow, methodical work, a lot of reading and experimenting, but the insights I'm gaining are invaluable. It's a different kind of problem-solving, and it's definitely contributing to my growth as a more well-rounded developer. Juggling these different types of projects keeps things fresh and prevents burnout.

Next up: For the Hebrew extension, maybe a minor dependency bump. For VisionMaker, I need to continue documenting and configuring the security agents, focusing on data ingestion and alerting.
